== Ethnic groups ==
{{main|Ethnic groups in Bosnia and Herzegovina|National minorities in Bosnia and Herzegovina}}
According to data from the [[2013 population census in Bosnia and Herzegovina|2013 census]] published by the Agency for Statistics of Bosnia and Herzegovina, [[Bosniaks]] constitute 50.11% of the population, [[Bosnian Serbs]] 30.78%, [[Bosnian Croats]] 15.43%, and others form 2.73%, with the remaining respondents not declaring their ethnicity or not answering.<ref name=Popis2013>{{cite web|title=Census of population, households and dwellings in Bosnia and Herzegovina, 2013: Final results|url=http://www.popis2013.ba/popis2013/doc/Popis2013prvoIzdanje.pdf|publisher=Agency for Statistics of Bosnia and Herzegovina|date=June 2016|access-date=1 July 2016|archive-date=30 June 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160630143615/http://www.popis2013.ba/popis2013/doc/Popis2013prvoIzdanje.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The census results are contested by the [[Republika Srpska]] statistical office and by Bosnian Serb politicians,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.balkaninsight.com/en/article/new-demographic-picture-of-bosnia-finally-revealed-06-30-2016|title=Census Reveals Bosnia's Changed Demography|first=Rodolfo|last=Toe|work=Balkan Insight|date=30 June 2016|access-date=1 July 2016}}</ref> who oppose the inclusion of non-permanent Bosnian residents in the figures.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.balkaninsight.com/en/article/bosnia-to-release-long-awaited-census-results-on-thursday-06-29-2016|title=Bosnia to Publish Census Without Serb Agreement|first=Rodolfo|last=Toe|work=Balkan Insight|date=30 June 2016|access-date=1 July 2016}}</ref>

The [[European Union]]'s statistics office, [[Eurostat]], determined that the methodology used by the Bosnian statistical agency was in line with international recommendations.<ref>{{cite web|title=Bosnia-Herzegovina has lost a fifth of its pre-war population|year=2016|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2016/jul/01/bosnia-herzegovina-has-lost-a-fifth-of-its-pre-war-population-census-shows|work=The Guardian}}</ref>

In Bosnia and Herzegovina, religion is often linked to ethnicity, i.e. (with the exception of [[Agnosticism|agnostics]] and [[Atheism|atheists]]) most Bosniaks are [[Muslim]], Serbs are [[Eastern Orthodox|Orthodox Christian]], and Croats are [[Roman Catholic]].

